  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nMidnight radar and satellite imagery depicted weakening showers\nand thunderstorms across NW KS, on the eastern periphery of the\nmonsoonal moisture plume, along the Colorado/Kansas border. It\nwas another clear very warm night across Kansas - still 85 at\nDDC at 1030 pm. \n\nPersistence will be the preferred forecasting method all the \nway through Labor Day weekend, with models showing little to no \nthermal variability from day to day. The stagnant summer pattern\nwill remain locked in through the weekend. Models do show \ncontinuity from previous solutions, with the core of the heat \ndome ridge remaining east of SW KS Tuesday and Wednesday, then \nretrograding westward to recenter over the SW KS region through \nthe holiday weekend. Can't rule out an isolated afternoon or \nevening thunderstorm at peak heating each day, but it will be \nexceedingly difficult for convection to mature or be widespread \nthrough the weekend in the strongly subsident environment. \nAnything that can form would probably favor the northwest zones \nnearest the monsoon's influence, but NBM pops are necessarily \nvery low. Most of SW KS will be dry for quite some time. \n\nNBM max temperature grids continue with their 1-3 degree cool\nbias, and added 1-3 degrees to the afternoon temperatures for\nthe next several days. Afternoon temperatures of 100-105 will be\ncommon each day through the weekend. The apparent temperature or\nheat index will flirt with heat advisory criteria (105) across\nthe southeast zones each afternoon, but in general this heat\nwave will feature a drier daily heat, with dewpoints in the\nupper 50s and lower 60s, with apparent temperatures remaining\nsimilar to the air temperatures. South winds will prevail each\nday, but do not appear overly strong on any given day, as lee \ntrough remains persistent but modest.",
